1. What is ASP.NET Core, and how is it different from ASP.NET MVC?

Answer:

ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform, high-performance framework developed by Microsoft for building modern web applications, REST APIs, microservices, and web services.

Unlike the older ASP.NET MVC Framework, ASP.NET Core can run on:

Windows
Linux
macOS

It is also designed around middleware, dependency injection, configuration, and built-in logging.

For example, a basic ASP.NET Core application starts from Program.cs:

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseStaticFiles();

app.UseRouting();

app.UseAuthorization();

app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");

app.Run();

In traditional ASP.NET MVC, we commonly worked with files such as:

Global.asax
Web.config
RouteConfig.cs
Startup.cs

In modern ASP.NET Core, much of this configuration is centralized in Program.cs.

Interview point:
ASP.NET Core is not simply a newer version of ASP.NET MVC. It is a redesigned, cross-platform framework with a middleware-based architecture and built-in dependency injection.

2. What is Dependency Injection in .NET Core?

Answer:

Dependency Injection, or DI, is a design pattern used to provide an object with the dependencies it needs instead of allowing the object to create those dependencies itself.

Suppose we have:

public interface IEmployeeService
{
    void AddEmployee();
}

Implementation:

public class EmployeeService : IEmployeeService
{
    public void AddEmployee()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Employee added");
    }
}

Instead of doing this:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    private EmployeeService service = new EmployeeService();
}

we inject the dependency:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    private readonly IEmployeeService _service;

    public EmployeeController(IEmployeeService service)
    {
        _service = service;
    }
}

Then register it in Program.cs:

builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();

Now ASP.NET Core's DI container automatically creates EmployeeService and passes it to the controller.

Why do we use DI?

It provides:

Loose coupling
Better maintainability
Better testability
Easier replacement of implementations
Centralized dependency management

For example, during unit testing we can replace:

IEmployeeService
       ↓
EmployeeService

with:

IEmployeeService
       ↓
MockEmployeeService

Interview answer:
"Dependency Injection allows us to inject dependencies into a class rather than creating them manually, which promotes loose coupling and makes applications easier to test and maintain."

3. Explain the different DI lifetimes in .NET Core.

Answer:

ASP.NET Core provides three major service lifetimes:

1. Transient
builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailService, EmailService>();

A new instance is created every time the service is requested.

Example:

Request
 ├── Service A → Instance 1
 ├── Service B → Instance 2
 └── Service C → Instance 3

Useful for lightweight stateless services.

2. Scoped
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();

One instance is created per HTTP request.

For example:

Request 1 → EmployeeService Instance 1
Request 2 → EmployeeService Instance 2
Request 3 → EmployeeService Instance 3

This is commonly used with:

DbContext

because we generally want one DbContext per request.

3. Singleton
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IConfigurationService, ConfigurationService>();

Only one instance is created for the application's lifetime.

Application
     ↓
One Service Instance
     ↓
All Requests

Useful for shared, thread-safe services such as certain caching or configuration services.

Important interview point

You should be careful about injecting a Scoped service into a Singleton, because the scoped service has a shorter lifetime.

A common rule is:

Transient → shortest
Scoped    → request lifetime
Singleton → application lifetime
4. What is Middleware in ASP.NET Core?

Answer:

Middleware is a component that participates in the HTTP request-response pipeline.

A request passes through multiple middleware components before reaching the endpoint.

For example:

Browser
   ↓
Exception Middleware
   ↓
HTTPS Middleware
   ↓
Static Files
   ↓
Routing
   ↓
Authentication
   ↓
Authorization
   ↓
Controller
   ↓
Response

Example:

app.UseAuthentication();

app.UseAuthorization();

app.MapControllers();

We can also create custom middleware:

public class LoggingMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public LoggingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Request received");

        await _next(context);

        Console.WriteLine("Response generated");
    }
}

Register it:

app.UseMiddleware<LoggingMiddleware>();

The important concept is:

await _next(context);

This passes control to the next middleware.

If you don't call _next, the pipeline can stop there.

5. Explain the ASP.NET Core MVC request lifecycle.

Answer:

This is a very important interview question.

Suppose the user requests:

/Employee/Details/10

The lifecycle is approximately:

HTTP Request
      ↓
Kestrel/Web Server
      ↓
Middleware Pipeline
      ↓
Routing
      ↓
Controller Selection
      ↓
Controller Creation
      ↓
Model Binding
      ↓
Action Filters
      ↓
Controller Action
      ↓
Business/Service Layer
      ↓
Database
      ↓
Action Result
      ↓
View Rendering
      ↓
HTTP Response

For example:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Details(int id)
    {
        var employee = _service.GetEmployee(id);

        return View(employee);
    }
}

When:

/Employee/Details/10

is requested, routing determines:

Controller = Employee
Action     = Details
id         = 10

Then the framework creates the controller through DI.

The id parameter is populated through model binding.

The action executes:

Details(10)

The service gets the employee from the database.

Finally:

return View(employee);

causes Razor to generate HTML.

The generated HTML is returned to the browser.

6. What is ASP.NET Core Web API?

Answer:

ASP.NET Core Web API is used to build HTTP-based services that allow different applications to communicate with each other.

For example:

React
Angular
Mobile App
Another Backend
       ↓
ASP.NET Core Web API
       ↓
Database

A controller can look like:

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class EmployeeController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult GetEmployees()
    {
        var employees = _service.GetEmployees();

        return Ok(employees);
    }
}

Calling:

GET /api/Employee

might return:

[
    {
        "id": 1,
        "name": "Rahul"
    },
    {
        "id": 2,
        "name": "Amit"
    }
]

Common HTTP methods are:

GET     → Retrieve
POST    → Create
PUT     → Update entire resource
PATCH   → Partial update
DELETE  → Delete

Web APIs normally return:

JSON

rather than Razor HTML views.

7. What is the difference between Controller and ControllerBase?

Answer:

In ASP.NET Core:

Controller

is generally used for MVC applications where we need Razor Views.

Example:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return View();
    }
}

Controller provides functionality such as:

View()
ViewBag
ViewData
TempData

For Web APIs, we generally use:

ControllerBase

Example:

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class EmployeeController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Get()
    {
        return Ok();
    }
}

ControllerBase provides API-related functionality such as:

Ok()
BadRequest()
NotFound()
Created()
Unauthorized()
Simple interview answer
MVC application → Controller
Web API          → ControllerBase

Although Controller itself derives from ControllerBase.

8. What is Routing in ASP.NET Core?

Answer:

Routing determines which controller/action or endpoint should handle an incoming HTTP request.

There are two common approaches:

Conventional Routing
app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");

So:

/Employee/Details/10

maps to:

EmployeeController
        ↓
Details()
        ↓
id = 10
Attribute Routing

We can define routes directly on controllers:

[ApiController]
[Route("api/employees")]
public class EmployeeController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult GetEmployee(int id)
    {
        return Ok();
    }
}

Now:

GET /api/employees/10

calls:

GetEmployee(10)

Attribute routing is very common in Web APIs.

9. What is Model Binding in ASP.NET Core?

Answer:

Model binding automatically converts incoming HTTP data into .NET objects or parameters.

Suppose the request is:

/Employee/Details/10

Controller:

public IActionResult Details(int id)
{
    return View();
}

ASP.NET Core automatically converts:

"10"

into:

int id = 10;

Another example:

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create(Employee employee)
{
    // employee is populated automatically
}

If the request contains:

{
    "name": "Rahul",
    "salary": 50000
}

ASP.NET Core creates:

Employee employee

and populates its properties.

This process is called model binding.

10. What is Model Validation?

Answer:

Model validation verifies whether incoming data satisfies the rules defined for the model.

Example:

public class Employee
{
    [Required]
    public string Name { get; set; }

    [Range(18, 60)]
    public int Age { get; set; }

    [EmailAddress]
    public string Email { get; set; }
}

If the user sends:

Name = empty
Age = 10
Email = invalid

validation will fail.

In MVC:

if (!ModelState.IsValid)
{
    return View(employee);
}

In Web API, when using:

[ApiController]

ASP.NET Core automatically performs model validation and can return:

400 Bad Request

with validation errors.

11. What is Entity Framework Core?

Answer:

Entity Framework Core, or EF Core, is an Object-Relational Mapper (ORM).

It allows us to work with a database using C# objects instead of writing SQL for every operation.

Suppose we have:

public class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public string Name { get; set; }

    public decimal Salary { get; set; }
}

And:

public class AppDbContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Employee> Employees { get; set; }
}

We can retrieve employees:

var employees = await _context.Employees.ToListAsync();

Instead of manually writing:

SELECT * FROM Employees;

EF Core generates the SQL.

For insertion:

_context.Employees.Add(employee);

await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

For deletion:

_context.Employees.Remove(employee);

await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

This makes database programming much easier and supports LINQ.

12. What is DbContext and why is it registered as Scoped?

Answer:

DbContext represents a session with the database.

It is responsible for:

Database connection management
Tracking entities
Querying
Saving changes
Managing relationships
Translating LINQ into SQL

Example:

public class AppDbContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Employee> Employees { get; set; }
}

Registration:

builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(connectionString));

AddDbContext() registers the context as Scoped by default.

That means one DbContext instance is normally used during one HTTP request.

For example:

Request 1
   ↓
DbContext 1

Request 2
   ↓
DbContext 2

This is useful because DbContext is designed to represent a short-lived unit of work and isn't generally intended to be shared across concurrent requests.

13. What are Migrations in EF Core?

Answer:

Migrations allow us to keep the database schema synchronized with our C# entity models.

Suppose we initially have:

public class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

Then we add:

public decimal Salary { get; set; }

We can create a migration:

Add-Migration AddSalaryToEmployee

Then apply it:

Update-Database

In CLI:

dotnet ef migrations add AddSalaryToEmployee
dotnet ef database update

The migration contains instructions describing the schema change.

Conceptually:

C# Model
   ↓
Migration
   ↓
Database Schema

If later we add another property:

Department

we create another migration.

Migrations are useful for version-controlling database schema changes along with application code.

14. What is the difference between Authentication and Authorization?

Answer:

This is one of the most common interview questions.

Authentication

Authentication answers:

Who are you?

For example:

Username + Password
       ↓
Login
       ↓
Identity verified
Authorization

Authorization answers:

What are you allowed to do?

For example:

Admin
 ↓
Create Employee
Delete Employee
Update Employee

Normal User
 ↓
View Employee

Example:

[Authorize]
public IActionResult Dashboard()
{
    return View();
}

Role-based authorization:

[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]
public IActionResult DeleteEmployee(int id)
{
    // Only Admin
}

So:

Authentication → Identity
Authorization  → Permission
15. What are Filters in ASP.NET Core MVC?

Answer:

Filters allow us to execute logic at specific stages of MVC request processing.

Common filter types include:

Authorization Filter
Resource Filter
Action Filter
Exception Filter
Result Filter

For example, an action filter can execute code before and after an action.

public class LoggingFilter : IActionFilter
{
    public void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Before action");
    }

    public void OnActionExecuted(ActionExecutedContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("After action");
    }
}

We can apply it:

[ServiceFilter(typeof(LoggingFilter))]
public IActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}

Filters are useful for cross-cutting concerns such as:

Logging
Validation
Authorization
Exception handling
Auditing
Middleware vs Filters

A useful interview distinction:

Middleware → Entire HTTP pipeline
Filter     → MVC/controller/action pipeline
16. What is async/await in ASP.NET Core and why is it important?

Answer:

async and await are used for asynchronous programming.

This is especially important for I/O operations such as:

Database calls
HTTP calls
File operations
External API calls

Example:

public async Task<IActionResult> GetEmployees()
{
    var employees = await _context.Employees.ToListAsync();

    return Ok(employees);
}

Instead of blocking a thread while the database is processing the request, asynchronous programming allows the application to use resources more efficiently.

Bad approach:

var employees = _context.Employees.ToList();

Preferred for I/O:

var employees = await _context.Employees.ToListAsync();
Important interview point

async/await does not automatically make database execution faster.

Its main benefit in web applications is improving scalability by avoiding unnecessary thread blocking during asynchronous I/O operations.

17. What is Configuration in ASP.NET Core?

Answer:

ASP.NET Core provides a configuration system that allows applications to read settings from different sources.

Common sources include:

appsettings.json
appsettings.Development.json
Environment Variables
Command-line arguments
User Secrets
External configuration providers

Example:

{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=.;Database=EmployeeDB;Trusted_Connection=True;"
  }
}

We can access it through:

var connectionString =
    builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection");

Or inject IConfiguration:

public class EmployeeService
{
    private readonly IConfiguration _configuration;

    public EmployeeService(IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _configuration = configuration;
    }
}

For strongly typed settings, we can use the Options pattern:

builder.Services.Configure<EmailSettings>(
    builder.Configuration.GetSection("EmailSettings"));

This is better than hardcoding values directly inside application code.

18. Explain the difference between IEnumerable, IQueryable, and List.

Answer:

This is particularly important when working with EF Core.

IEnumerable

Generally represents an in-memory sequence and operations are typically performed against objects already loaded into memory.

Example:

IEnumerable<Employee> employees =
    _context.Employees.ToList();

The database query has already executed because of ToList().

IQueryable

Represents a query that can be translated by a provider such as EF Core into SQL.

IQueryable<Employee> employees =
    _context.Employees;

var result = employees
    .Where(x => x.Salary > 50000)
    .ToList();

The filtering can be translated into SQL and executed by the database.

Conceptually:

IQueryable
   ↓
LINQ expression
   ↓
SQL
   ↓
Database
List

List<T> is an in-memory collection.

List<Employee> employees =
    await _context.Employees.ToListAsync();

At this point, the records are loaded into application memory.

Interview summary
IQueryable → Query can be translated/executed by provider
IEnumerable → Sequence you can enumerate
List       → Concrete in-memory collection
19. What is the Repository Pattern and why is it used?

Answer:

The Repository Pattern creates an abstraction between application/business logic and the data-access mechanism.

Instead of directly writing database code inside the controller:

public IActionResult GetEmployees()
{
    var employees = _context.Employees.ToList();

    return View(employees);
}

we can use:

Controller
    ↓
Service
    ↓
Repository
    ↓
DbContext
    ↓
Database

Interface:

public interface IEmployeeRepository
{
    Task<List<Employee>> GetAllAsync();

    Task<Employee> GetByIdAsync(int id);

    Task AddAsync(Employee employee);
}

Implementation:

public class EmployeeRepository : IEmployeeRepository
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _context;

    public EmployeeRepository(AppDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    public async Task<List<Employee>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Employees.ToListAsync();
    }
}

Register it:

builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeRepository, EmployeeRepository>();

Then controller:

public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    private readonly IEmployeeRepository _repository;

    public EmployeeController(IEmployeeRepository repository)
    {
        _repository = repository;
    }
}

This gives us loose coupling and makes testing easier.

Important interview nuance: EF Core's DbSet and DbContext already provide repository/unit-of-work-like behavior, so adding a repository layer is a design choice, not an absolute requirement.

20. Explain the complete architecture of a real ASP.NET Core application.

Answer:

A real-world ASP.NET Core application is often divided into multiple layers.

For example:

                Client
                  ↓
             Controller/API
                  ↓
              Service
                  ↓
             Repository
                  ↓
              DbContext
                  ↓
              Database

Let's take an Employee application.

Controller

Responsible for handling HTTP requests:

[HttpGet]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetEmployee(int id)
{
    var employee = await _service.GetEmployee(id);

    if (employee == null)
        return NotFound();

    return Ok(employee);
}
Service

Contains business logic:

public async Task<Employee> GetEmployee(int id)
{
    return await _repository.GetByIdAsync(id);
}
Repository

Handles data access:

public async Task<Employee> GetByIdAsync(int id)
{
    return await _context.Employees
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(x => x.Id == id);
}
DbContext

Communicates with the database:

public class AppDbContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Employee> Employees { get; set; }
}
DI

Connects all these layers:

builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeRepository, EmployeeRepository>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();

So the complete flow becomes:

HTTP Request
      ↓
Middleware
      ↓
Routing
      ↓
Controller
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Repository
      ↓
DbContext
      ↓
SQL Server
      ↓
DbContext
      ↓
Repository
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Controller
      ↓
HTTP Response

For an MVC application, the final step might instead be:

Controller
    ↓
View Model
    ↓
Razor View
    ↓
HTML
    ↓
Browser

For a Web API:

Controller
    ↓
Object
    ↓
JSON Serialization
    ↓
HTTP Response
